/></highlight
><text color="#9643FC" creationdate="D:20250522092454+10'00'" flags="print,nozoom,norotate" inreplyto="449cb013-4466-4a71-8032-d5e36d876188" date="D:20250522092454+10'00'" name="deb5145e-8c9f-45b7-a3bf-41071334dfb4" icon="Comment" page="8" rect="35.654300,327.833000,59.654300,351.833000" subject="Sticky Note" title="Alexander Gibson"
><contents-richtext
><body xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1999/xhtml" xmlns:xfa="http://www.xfa.org/schema/xfa-data/1.0/" xfa:APIVersion="Acrobat:25.1.0" xfa:spec="2.0.2"
><p dir="ltr"
><span dir="ltr" style="font-size:-14.0pt;text-align:left;font-weight:normal;font-style:normal"
>Q14c: </span
><span dir="ltr" style="font-size:-13.3pt;text-align:left;font-weight:normal;font-style:normal"
>Like other audited areas, women are underrepresented in studies&#xD;examining different approaches to CHO in the everyday training&#xD;diet, and the available research is limited by poor methodological&#xD;design in the control of ovarian hormones, and the implementation&#xD;and verification of chronic dietary interventions. Future research on&#xD;CHO support for training should use Best Practice Guidelines for&#xD;the conduct and reporting of these aspects of research. Specific&#xD;studies of female athletes, including direct investigation of potential&#xD;sex differences in response to manipulation of CHO support for&#xD;training when corrected and normalized for fat-free mass compared&#xD;with males, are needed to develop evidence-based guidelines that&#xD;cover the whole of the athletic population.</span
></p
